{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791048924,"nonce":"1Aun/jGj/w+RLmed+uHZaWSoOks=","title":"Convenience functions: signal(value) and computed(() -\u003e ...)","message":"Making reactive values reads Signal.make(0) and Computed.make(() -\u003e a.get() * 2). Offer the short spellings the user asked for, as SolidJS's createSignal / createMemo read in practice:\n\n    var count = signal(0);\n    var doubled = computed(() -\u003e count.get() * 2);\n\nShape to check: Haxe 4.2+ has module-level functions, so a module such as ashui.reactive.Reactive (or a top-level ashui.Reactive) can hold `signal` and `computed`, imported with `import ashui.reactive.Reactive.*`. Signal.make is a macro that picks the signal kind from the value's type (Int, Float, Bool, String, Color, …); the shorthand must keep that, so `signal` should be a macro too (check that a module-level macro function works on the compiler in use; if not, use a class with static macro functions and import its fields). computed can forward to Computed.make. Also consider watch(read, react) for Watch, and making hxx templates and the docs' examples use the short forms. Smoke checks: signal(0) is an Int signal, signal(new Color(…)) a colour one, computed tracks.","files":null}]}